aept (0.8.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: "aept verify" checks the files of the named packages -- of every
    installed package when none is named -- against what was recorded
    when they were installed: presence, type, permission bits, owner and
    group, a symlink's target, and a regular file's size and SHA-256.
    One line per difference, naming the kind, the path, the package and,
    where it applies, what was recorded and what was found. What is
    recorded decides what is checked: a column an older list lacks is
    not held against anything, and a regular file with no digest at all
    is reported as "unverifiable" rather than passed, so a root from
    before digests were recorded is not silently clean. A conffile is
    compared against the hash its conffile record keeps and reported as
    "conffile", which is not damage. Neither of those two counts as a
    failure: the command exits 0 when nothing is damaged, 1 when
    something is, and 2 on an error, including a named package that is
    not installed, so it can gate a health check. Owner and group are
    not checked under "option ignore_ownership"; "--ignore-ownership"
    does the same for one run. Repair is "aept install --reinstall",
    which restores every kind but the conffile.
  * A directory with a filesystem mounted on it is skipped whole: on a
    live root /proc and /sys are kernel-owned 0555 whatever base-files
    shipped, and the first run against a real target reported exactly
    those two.
  * ABI: aept_verify(), aept_verify_list_free(), the AEPT_VERIFY_*
    kinds and the aept_verify_entry_t and aept_verify_list_t types are
    added; nothing is removed or changed. libaept's interface version
    is 1:2:0. The Python bindings gain Context.verify(), VerifyEntry and
    VerifyKind, whose is_damage property tells the two reported-only
    kinds from the rest.
  * Tests: test_integrity.sh produces each kind of damage one at a time
    and compares the whole reported line and the exit status, then
    proves the reinstall repairs all of them but the conffile.
    test_verify.c now fails fork() through the linker rather than
    RLIMIT_NPROC, which root is exempt from: under the package build,
    which runs the suite as root, the fork went ahead and the failure
    branch stayed unreached, tripping the coverage ratchet in CI against
    a baseline blessed as a user.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 13:17:30 +0300

aept (0.7.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Change: every file is written aside and renamed into place. A regular
    file, symlink or hard link is extracted as "<path>.aept-new" and
    renamed over its path once it is whole, so the path holds the old
    object or the complete new one and never part of either: a write
    that fails half-way, for want of space or by a kill, leaves the old
    file where it was and the partial copy is removed. Directories are
    still made in place. This is dpkg's per-file atomicity, not its
    per-package unwind -- a package whose tenth file fails leaves its
    first nine installed.
  * Fix: a package that did not fit on the filesystem installed without
    error. libarchive reports a failed write with the same warning code
    it uses for a timestamp it could not restore, and then extends the
    file to the size the entry declared, so a package written to a full
    disk came out as files of the right length with holes where the data
    should be. aept now writes regular-file data itself and fails on the
    first short write. libarchive's own aside-and-rename mode was
    measured and does the same: the temporary is renamed over the
    original regardless of the failed write.
  * Fix: two things libarchive did on its own are refused: removing an
    empty directory to put a file in its place, and reporting success
    when a regular file stood where a directory entry lands. Device
    nodes, FIFOs and sockets in a package are refused, at the clash check
    before any script runs and again at extraction.
  * New: a package's shipped sha256sums -- the control member the Aeltra
    build tool writes -- is checked at extraction, file by file and before
    the rename, so a file whose digest does not match never goes into
    place. A file in the archive the list lacks, a file in the list the
    archive lacks, and a list that is there but cannot be read all refuse
    the package. This is the first check in the chain -- signed index,
    package digest, gzip CRC -- that reaches the file itself. A package
    that ships no list, which is every package in the current archive,
    installs as before and aept's own digest is recorded.
  * Change: the file list, /var/lib/aept/info/<pkg>.list, records the
    mode, owner, group, size and sha256 of every file beside its path,
    tab separated, with a symlink's target last and "-" for a column
    that does not apply. Lists written by earlier versions still read;
    what they do not record reads as unknown. The digest is the shipped
    one, or that of what was written when the package shipped none.
    Files are hashed with OpenSSL, which is twice as fast as libsolv's
    portable sha256 on a machine without SHA instructions and several
    times faster on one with them; it is the install's dominant cost.
  * Change: "option ignore_uid" is now "option ignore_ownership", and
    means something different. ignore_uid skipped restoring ownership
    altogether and left every file owned by whoever ran aept.
    ignore_ownership says that a failure to restore it is expected: aept
    always tries, so inside a user namespace the owners that are mapped
    are set, and a file that cannot be given to the owner the package
    names is kept as it is, minus its setuid and setgid bits, which would
    otherwise grant an identity the package did not mean. Without the
    option such a failure is now an error whose message names the
    option; before, a failed chown was accepted with a debug message.
    Two environments need it: a root assembled by an unprivileged user,
    and root inside a user namespace that does not map every owner a
    package names, where chown fails with EINVAL however root the caller
    looks. The recorded owner is always the one on disk.
  * ABI: AEPT_FLAG_IGNORE_UID is renamed AEPT_FLAG_IGNORE_OWNERSHIP in
    place; its value and the enum's size are unchanged, so a compiled
    caller is unaffected and a source using the old name has one rename
    to make. The Python bindings expose Flag.IGNORE_OWNERSHIP in place of
    Flag.IGNORE_UID. libaept's interface version is 1:1:1.
  * Tests: test_atomic_unpack.sh fills a tmpfs mid-extraction;
    test_sha256sums.sh and test_sums.c cover the shipped digests and
    every refusal; test_unprivileged_owner.sh covers both environments
    with and without the option; test_listfile.c pins every shape of
    list line.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 09:54:48 +0300

aept (0.6.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Change: maintainer scripts run in the order dpkg runs them, and a
    failed step is unwound. The file-clash check now runs before any
    script, so a refused install has no prerm or preinst to undo. On an
    upgrade the old postrm runs before the files the new version dropped
    are deleted, not after. A preinst or unpack that fails is followed by
    "postrm abort-install", or "postrm abort-upgrade" and then the old
    version's "postinst abort-upgrade" -- the call that restarts what the
    old prerm stopped. An old prerm that fails gives the new version's
    "prerm failed-upgrade" a chance before the upgrade is abandoned; an
    old postrm that fails gives "postrm failed-upgrade" one and carries
    on; a "prerm remove" that fails is followed by "postinst
    abort-remove". "preinst upgrade" now receives both versions. None of
    these calls were made before.
  * Change: unpacking and configuring are separate steps, and a package
    that a new package both conflicts with and replaces is removed in
    between: after the new package is unpacked, so its files can be taken
    over, and before its postinst runs, so the daemon it starts does not
    find the old one still on the port. Previously the new package was
    configured first and the old one removed afterwards. If the old
    package's prerm refuses, the new one is left unpacked rather than
    configured beside a package it conflicts with. A package is recorded
    as "unpacked" from the moment its files are on disk and as
    "installed" once its postinst has run.
  * Change: a file taken over under "Replaces" leaves the old owner's
    file list as soon as it is on disk, whether or not the new package's
    postinst then succeeds. It used to wait for the postinst, so a failed
    one left the old owner still claiming a file that was not its, and
    removing that owner later deleted it. A package whose last file is
    taken over disappears before the overwriter is configured, not after,
    and does not disappear at all while another installed package depends
    on or recommends it -- it stays, owning nothing.
  * Fix: extraction failed to fail when an entry could not be created
    because a regular file stood where its parent directory should be.
    libarchive reports that with the same warning code it uses for a
    timestamp it could not restore, and the install completed with a file
    list naming a file that was never written. A warning is now accepted
    only if the entry is actually on disk.
  * New: "aept mark protected". A protected package cannot be removed:
    not by name, not to satisfy a conflict, not as a dependant of
    something else being removed, and not by an upgrade that would have
    to drop it -- such a transaction is refused as a whole, and a removal
    typed by name is refused in words. It can still be upgraded, and
    installing it again by name leaves it protected. There is no force
    flag; "aept mark manual" lifts the protection. Marks apply to real
    package names: to keep "some provider of init", protect a package
    that depends on init. The C API gains aept_mark_protected() and the
    Python bindings mark_protected().
  * Change: the marks are kept in one file, /var/lib/aept/marks, one
    "name mark" line per package that is auto or protected; a package
    with no line is manual. It replaces /var/lib/aept/auto-installed and
    the "auto_file" option, which is now "marks_file". There is no
    migration, since nothing has been distributed; a root assembled with
    an earlier aept converts with
      sed 's/$/ auto/' auto-installed > marks
    A damaged line costs that line and nothing else; a duplicated name
    reads by its first line and is written back once. The file is read
    once per API call, so a transaction marking each of its packages as
    it goes no longer re-reads it per package.
  * Change: libaept's interface version is 1:1:0 (one function added,
    none changed).
  * Tests: test_script_order.sh asserts the complete maintainer-script
    sequence for every path, including each failure point's unwind;
    test_protected.sh covers every route by which a protected package
    could otherwise be removed; test_marks.c the marks file.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Tue, 15 Sep 2026 01:18:24 +0300

aept (0.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Change: Recommends are no longer installed. libsolv satisfies them by
    default, which is apt's behaviour and the wrong one for a small root:
    a package pulled in whatever its recommends named, transitively, and
    nothing chose that, documented it, or could switch it off. Set
    "option install_recommends 1" for apt's behaviour. Suggests were never
    followed and still are not.
  * Change: "Conflicts: X" with "Replaces: X" no longer makes a package an
    update candidate for X. libsolv's Debian reader files Replaces under
    obsoletes, and to libsolv an obsoletes is a supersede -- so every mail
    transport agent was an upgrade path for every other, and a plain
    "aept upgrade" could swap one for another nobody asked for. The pair
    now means what Debian says it means: X must go for this package to be
    installed, and this package may take X's files on the way. That
    ordering -- install over, then remove what is left -- is aept's own,
    since libsolv orders a conflict's removal first.
  * New: "Replaces: X" on its own -- the file-move form, normally paired
    with a versioned Breaks on a package split -- is honoured rather than
    refused. Both packages stay installed, the path leaves X's file list
    so removing X later does not delete it, and a package left owning no
    files at all disappears: its postrm is called with "disappear
    <overwriter> <version>" and its records are cleared. Breaks is not
    read as a conflict for this purpose; a versioned Breaks is satisfied
    by moving the other package on, as the field asks.
  * New: aept parses package indexes itself, through libsolv's own
    solvable API, instead of calling libsolvext's Debian reader. That
    reader is what folded Replaces into obsoletes, and owning the parse
    is what lets Replaces and Conflicts be kept as the packager wrote
    them. It accepts the binary-package grammar -- names, version
    relations, alternatives -- and refuses what it cannot represent,
    dropping the package rather than silently weakening a dependency.
    Verified field-for-field against the archive index and against
    Debian trixie's 68,825 packages. libsolvext is no longer needed to
    build or run aept.
  * Fix: version comparison followed whatever libsolv had been built for.
    Debian's package chooses Debian semantics; an upstream cmake build
    defaults to RPM's, which orders 726 of 23,259 real Debian version
    pairs differently -- "1.0.1-1" against "1.0+2-1" is an upgrade to one
    and a downgrade to the other. aept now asks for Debian semantics and
    refuses to start against a libsolv that cannot provide them.
  * Fix: "aept mark manual --all" died in the subcommand's option parser
    on glibc and worked on musl. Dispatching parsers stop at the first
    non-option and leaf parsers permute; both reset with optind = 0.
  * Fix: the signal handler could read a context that aept_cleanup() had
    already freed.
  * Fix: aept_architectures() returned -1 with no error recorded, and
    handed back an array with a NULL in it when strdup() failed. api.c
    now uses the allocators that cannot fail.
  * Fix: aept show reported Installed-Size in bytes as kB, printed the
    description twice, and named fields as libsolv had rendered them.
    It now prints the candidate version (every version under -a), reads
    the relationship fields from the control stanza as the packager
    wrote them, and adds Section, Source, Maintainer and Download-Size.
    aept list agrees with install on which version is the candidate.
  * Fix: a download that succeeded erased the classification of one that
    had failed earlier in the same call, so an update that lost a source
    to a timeout reported nothing in particular. aept_last_error() is
    now cleared once, when a call is entered, and set only by failures.
    An update that lost any source reports AEPT_ERR_GENERAL rather than
    passing up a per-source timeout that may have happened a successful
    source ago, and whose choice depended on the order of the sources.
  * New: libaept no longer ends its embedder's process on allocation
    failure. A failed allocation unwinds to the public entry point, which
    returns -1 with AEPT_ERR_NOMEM; the Python bindings raise
    AeptNoMemory. Partial state is not rolled back, only the process
    survives.
  * New: "option clean_cache 0" makes clean leave the cache directory
    alone, for a cache shared with something outside the root.
  * New: "aept show" takes -a/--all to print every offered version.
  * Tests: every relationship field has a contract test, every public
    entry point is swept for allocation and I/O failure at every point,
    and the suite runs under musl in a container (scripts/musl-build.sh),
    which is how the version-comparison bug was found. The package build
    runs the format and coverage gates.
  * ABI: libaept goes from 0:3:0 to 1:0:0 (libaept1). aept_set_offline_root()
    and aept_set_cache_dir() return int, being the only setters that can
    fail; aept_pkg_info_t gained section, source, maintainer and
    download_size; aept_show_all(), aept_pkg_info_list_t and
    AEPT_ERR_NOMEM are added. The Python bindings expose the new fields,
    show_all(), and AeptNoMemory.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 11:40:29 +0300

aept (0.4.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: the connection cache never engaged against an HTTP/1.1 server
    that says nothing about persistence -- which is what nginx and
    Caddy do, since RFC 9112 9.3 makes persistence the default and a
    server signals it by staying silent. libfetch started from
    HTTP/1.0's rule and turned reuse on only for an explicit
    Connection: keep-alive, so every index and every package paid a
    fresh name lookup and a fresh TLS handshake. The default now comes
    from the reply's version. Measured over five downloads of one URL:
    10 connect() calls and 5 DNS queries before, 2 and 1 after.
  * Fix: Connection is a token list, so "keep-alive, TE" and "TE,
    close" are both legal and neither was recognised by comparing the
    whole field value against one token. It is parsed as a list now,
    with close winning when a peer names both.
  * No ABI or Python API change; libaept stays at 0:3:0.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 19:20:41 +0300

aept (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: the cache directory can now also be set via the AEPT_CACHE_DIR
    environment variable, in addition to -C/--cache-dir on the command
    line. Precedence: --cache-dir > AEPT_CACHE_DIR > option cache_dir >
    default. This lets a wrapper set the override once and have it
    apply to aept invocations it does not launch itself -- e.g.
    bootstrap scripts run against an offline root.
  * No ABI or Python API change; libaept stays at 0:3:0.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 10:28:06 +0300

aept (0.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Packaging: the 0.3.0 release left configure.ac at 0.2.0, so the
    version check in debian/rules refused the build and 0.3.0 never
    produced a package -- which is the check doing its job, since the
    binary would otherwise have reported aept/0.2.0 as its User-Agent
    to every repository it fetched from. configure.ac and
    python/pyproject.toml now carry the release version. No source
    change otherwise; libaept stays at 0:3:0, which no release ever
    shipped.

